# SAMPLE FOR A TARGET PROJECT — NOT ACTIVE, NOT THIS REPOSITORY'S CI.
#
# This file is part of the `make` reference Skill's samples (skills/make/samples/ci/).
# It is a template for a project that ADOPTS this Skill's `make verify` (or `make check`)
# command contract — it has nothing to do with, and is never read by, this repository's
# own CI (this repository does not use Azure Pipelines at all).
#
# It is inert by construction: the `.yml.example` extension is not a filename Azure
# Pipelines looks for, and this path (`skills/make/samples/ci/azure-pipelines.yml.example`)
# is not registered as any pipeline's YAML file in any Azure DevOps project, so nothing
# will ever execute it as-is.
#
# To use it in a target project:
#   1. Copy it to that project's chosen pipeline path (commonly `azure-pipelines.yml` at
#      the repository root), drop `.example`, and register it as a pipeline in Azure
#      DevOps (Pipelines > New pipeline > Existing Azure Pipelines YAML file).
#   2. Make sure that project's Makefile actually defines a `verify` target (see this
#      Skill's `references/architecture/command-contracts.md` for the contract shape).
#      If it only defines `check`, change the `script:` line below to `make check`.
#   3. Adjust `trigger` / `pr` branches and `pool.vmImage` to that project's conventions.
#
# What this file is and is not:
#   - It is a pipeline DEFINITION only (what runs). It does NOT configure Azure DevOps
#     branch policies (whether a run is REQUIRED to complete before merge) — that is a
#     separate, host-side setting configured under Repos > Branches > Branch policies,
#     analogous to the GitHub rulesets vs. workflow-file split described in
#     `references/quality/ci-hooks-and-cache.md`. Registering this pipeline does not, by
#     itself, make it a required check.
#   - It assumes GNU Make is already present on the agent image (true for Microsoft-hosted
#     `ubuntu-latest` agents at the time of writing). If a different agent image or a
#     self-hosted agent lacks `make`, add an explicit install step before the `make
#     verify` step and record the required version, per
#     `references/maintenance/troubleshooting.md`.
#   - It intentionally calls one existing entry point (`make verify`) rather than
#     inlining fmt/lint/test/build steps here, so a Git hook, this pipeline, and a
#     developer's local invocation all resolve to the same definition (see
#     `references/quality/ci-hooks-and-cache.md`, "One command contract, two callers").
#
# This is a sample, not an endorsement to add CI to any specific project. Adding or
# changing a project's real CI is an `apply`-phase action requiring the project owner's
# explicit approval — see this Skill's SKILL.md and `references/architecture/command-contracts.md`.

trigger:
  branches:
    include:
      - main

pr:
  branches:
    include:
      - main

pool:
  vmImage: ubuntu-latest

steps:
  - checkout: self

  - script: make verify
    displayName: 'Run verify'
